WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.862
How can the same set of pins have three different wirelengths?

00:00:03.862 --> 00:00:09.300
In this lesson, we use three different rulers to understand net models in chip
placement.

00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:14.117
A net on a chip can connect several pins at once.

00:00:14.117 --> 00:00:18.967
The connections are already specified; placement chooses their positions.

00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:23.617
Before estimating wirelength, we must also choose a measurement model.

00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:28.792
Start with four pins at the corners of a square of side two.

00:00:28.792 --> 00:00:34.579
We use these same coordinates throughout, comparing a bounding box, a
clique, and a star.

00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:39.896
We use Manhattan distance, rather than straight-line distance.

00:00:39.896 --> 00:00:44.733
Add the absolute horizontal difference to the absolute vertical difference.

00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:48.346
The bent lines in our drawings illustrate that measurement.

00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:55.558
From A at the lower left to D at the upper right, move two units horizontally
and two vertically.

00:00:55.558 --> 00:00:58.121
Their Manhattan distance is four.

00:00:58.121 --> 00:01:01.633
Every model here uses this same basic distance.

00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:07.246
The first model finds the smallest box containing all pins.

00:01:07.246 --> 00:01:10.858
The leftmost and rightmost pins determine its width.

00:01:10.858 --> 00:01:13.921
The lowest and highest determine its height.

00:01:13.921 --> 00:01:15.883
Add width and height.

00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:23.192
For our square, width and height are both two, giving a half-perimeter
wirelength of four.

00:01:23.192 --> 00:01:26.417
Walking around all four sides gives eight.

00:01:26.417 --> 00:01:29.767
That is the full perimeter, not this metric.

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:34.692
The bounding box needs only four extreme coordinates.

00:01:34.692 --> 00:01:40.567
Scan each pin once to find them, so the computation grows linearly with the
number of pins.

00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:43.254
It is a cheap wirelength estimate.

00:01:44.533 --> 00:01:51.046
The second model is a clique: connect every pair of distinct pins and add
their Manhattan distances.

00:01:51.046 --> 00:01:54.146
Four pins give six unordered pairs.

00:01:54.146 --> 00:01:56.808
Count each pair exactly once.

00:01:58.067 --> 00:02:06.104
In alphabetical order, measure A to B, A to C, and A to D; then B to C, B to D,
and C to D.

00:02:06.104 --> 00:02:11.892
The six distances are two, two, four, four, two, and two.

00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:16.079
Adding these six terms gives sixteen.

00:02:16.079 --> 00:02:19.067
Do not count both A to B and B to A.

00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:25.042
Those names describe the same unordered pair; double counting would
incorrectly produce thirty-two.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:31.788
With p pins, a clique has p times p minus one, divided by two edges.

00:02:31.788 --> 00:02:35.200
Here we sum unnormalized Manhattan distances.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.575
This is not the squared-distance objective used in quadratic placement.

00:02:40.867 --> 00:02:43.242
The third model is a star.

00:02:43.242 --> 00:02:47.604
Choose one existing pin as a hub and connect it to every other pin.

00:02:47.604 --> 00:02:49.829
Our example uses A.

00:02:49.829 --> 00:02:52.679
We do not introduce a virtual center.

00:02:53.967 --> 00:02:58.929
With A as the hub, the spokes are A to B, A to C, and A to D.

00:02:58.929 --> 00:03:03.354
Their lengths are two, two, and four, totaling eight.

00:03:03.354 --> 00:03:06.329
Other pin pairs do not enter this sum.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:13.375
A star has only p minus one spokes, so its computation is linear in the pin
count.

00:03:13.375 --> 00:03:16.362
But the hub is part of the model definition.

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:19.175
Always name it when reporting the result.

00:03:20.467 --> 00:03:24.567
The same pins now give four, sixteen, and eight.

00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:28.104
Neither their coordinates nor their connections changed.

00:03:28.104 --> 00:03:30.467
Only the measurement changed.

00:03:30.467 --> 00:03:34.104
These numbers alone cannot rank which model is better.

00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:40.529
For the square, choosing any corner as the star hub happens to give the
same answer.

00:03:40.529 --> 00:03:43.254
That is a consequence of symmetry.

00:03:43.254 --> 00:03:47.029
An asymmetric example reveals why the hub matters.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:55.475
Using A, B, C, and D as hubs now gives sixteen, twenty-two, fourteen, and
twenty.

00:03:55.475 --> 00:03:58.162
C has the smallest value here.

00:03:58.162 --> 00:04:03.088
This shows hub dependence; it does not prove a placement algorithm is
optimal.

00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:09.454
Next, A, B, and C support an eight by eight bounding box.

00:04:09.454 --> 00:04:14.092
Move the interior pin D from four, four to two, two.

00:04:14.092 --> 00:04:17.567
Watch the three rulers: which readings change?

00:04:18.867 --> 00:04:23.542
The half-perimeter stays sixteen because the outer boundaries did not
change.

00:04:23.542 --> 00:04:29.192
The clique decreases from fifty-six to fifty-two, and the star from twenty-four
to twenty.

00:04:29.192 --> 00:04:32.704
Different models respond differently to the same move.

00:04:33.967 --> 00:04:38.767
Place every pin at one position and all three wirelengths become zero.

00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:42.304
Real cells have area and cannot freely overlap.

00:04:42.304 --> 00:04:46.767
These measurements have not checked density, overlap, or timing.

00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:53.396
The bent lines illustrate measurement relationships, not final shared routing
paths.

00:04:53.396 --> 00:05:00.383
Before comparing two placement candidates, align the model, the distance,
the weights, and the constraints.

00:05:00.383 --> 00:05:03.258
Small numbers need a clear definition.

00:05:04.533 --> 00:05:09.258
On the website, load the spread square and then the compact reference
square.

00:05:09.258 --> 00:05:15.546
The readings change from sixteen, sixty-four, thirty-two to four, sixteen,
eight.

00:05:15.546 --> 00:05:18.858
Drag a pin to investigate your own placement.

00:05:20.133 --> 00:05:25.708
Change the star hub, move the interior pin, and try the six experiment tasks.

00:05:25.708 --> 00:05:28.258
Write down your prediction first.

00:05:28.258 --> 00:05:33.408
Then inspect each computed contribution and explain where the change
came from.

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:41.800
Finally, implement the three functions in the downloadable Python exercise
and compare with the independent reference.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:48.575
More important than remembering four, sixteen, and eight is explaining
exactly what each number measures.
